        }
  // other methods like Clear,CopyTo, etc...

	}


}

Also, note that it is a good practice to ensure your assumptions hold true by invoking the appropriate `AssumePrecondition.IsNotNull(...)` or `AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(...)` in your test methods. It does not ensure that all methods will behave correctly, but it will make your tests more efficient by only running those methods that have preconditions that are satisfied. By utilizing this Pex method, you can create more focused tests.

### How to make this test harness work?

After adding these test harnesses, you can run the test suite on the project to generate test cases. The framework should generate input that is similar to your real-life program inputs and also test other edge cases which your program has.

### How to use the generated test cases?

You can run your program with the generated tests using the nunit runner. Make sure you install the nunit console runner. Once you run your test, it should execute the tests, and you would be able to see the results in the console.

Please note that you may need to adjust the code to your current programming style or to the actual project implementation since, above code examples are using a static array list class as it's the closest version in C# we can work with. A good practice is to mock the ArrayList class and write your tests that test individual behaviors or contracts.

This is a simple and basic example of how Pex can help you generate test cases. You can also write your test harness with any other static inputs or more complex scenarios depending on what you need. You can add as many methods as you want to test different scenarios in your ArrayList class. The important point here is to maintain a high quality test suite that ensures your code is working as expected.

