        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_RemoveAtContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_index = index;
            int Old_arrListAtIndex = (int)arrList[index];
            bool Old_arrListContainsIndexValue = arrList.Contains(arrList[index]);

            arrList.RemoveAt(index);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            bool New_arrListContainsIndexValue = arrList.Contains(Old_arrListAtIndex);
            int New_arrListIndexOfOldValue = arrList.IndexOf(Old_arrListAtIndex);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_At_index", Old_arrListAtIndex);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_index_value", Old_arrListContainsIndexValue);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_old_value", New_arrListContainsIndexValue);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_old_value", New_arrListIndexOfOldValue);


        }

    }
}

For the case where 'ArrayList arrList' is initialized through the initialization step, it is not shown as a parameter of the above Pex methods.
Also if the 'arrList' is initialized with elements, it should not be part of the Pex method parameter itself, but part of the assumption or initialization step.
In your actual code, you may have to initialize the list with some 'known values' to meet your test requirements.

As per your requirements, I'm initializing the ArrayList with 5 known items in the init step as follows:

[TestFixture]
public partial class ArrayListContractTest
{
    [PexMethod]
    public void PUT_AddContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
    {
        AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

        // Initialization step
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
        {
            arrList.Add(i);
        }

        // Your tests...
    }
}

Also, note that Pex will generate tests with and without null parameters, but in your test methods, always assume the parameters are not null. So you don't need to check for null before calling these methods. As per Microsoft's official documentation, "The PexAssert.IsNotNull method is not needed — all parameters will be considered non-null by Pex." You can leave this check or replace with AssumePrecondition.ForNonNullArguments.

For PexObserve.IsTrue, you simply need to verify that the conditions hold true before and after the method call. If the condition is true before the method call, it should remain true after the function execution, and vice versa. It is mainly used for logging the values during the execution to help understand if there's any unexpected behavior.

In your case, I am verifying, for example, if the Count property of the list increases after calling Add method. For this, you would add 'PexObserve.IsTrue(New_arrList_Count == Old_arrList_Count+1);' before you call Add method. You could do similar assertions for other methods such as Remove, Insert, and Set.

Additionally, Pex works best if there is a 'known-good' path of execution (i.e., a 'corner case' where no 'exception' behavior is triggered). This allows Pex to focus on exploring the 'breadth' of state-space.

Lastly, remember to use PexObserve.ValueForViewing to verify any return values or intermediate values. This helps in understanding what is going on in your tests, particularly in complicated scenarios. You can view these values, returned values, and other interesting information by looking at the test results in Pex Visual Studio. You can--------------------
	    Debug.Assert(arrList.Contains(New_Ret)); 
        }

