I am trying to use Pex to contract test a method that uses the above ArrayList object. However, when I try to run Pex, it doesn't find any test cases. Any help would be appreciated.

A: I suspect you are missing the "PexAllowedException" from the method and the "PexAssemblyUnderTest" Attribute from your Class.
Try this:
[TestFixture]
[PexClass(MethodReplacement = ReplacementStrategy.None)]
[PexAllowedException(typeof(NotImplementedException))]
[TargetType("System.Collections.ArrayList")]
public partial class ArrayListContractTest
{
    // ... your tests here ...
}

Note that the method signature should also be correct. It should not have exceptions and the return values should not exceed the specified count or any other conditions you have set in the parameters.
You may also need something like this for the type of the method you're trying to test:
[PexMethod(OutputBehavior = OutputBehavior.FuzzOnly, ExpectedException = typeof(NotImplementedException), StateBehavior = StateBehavior.Ideal)]

Note: The output behavior fuzz only is to run the test with generated input to make sure it still works with generated parameters and exceptions. If the test always outputs the given object but the test is able to find it, it is considered a failure.
Also, please make sure you are using the correct "Put" prefix for your methods, as per the naming conventions Pex uses.
Another possibility could be the size of the object. 
ArrayList can be quite large in sizes, if you try to create a test for such an arraylist, Pex may lead to an issue or a very long running time of the test. Consider creating smaller objects.
These are just possible reasons. Without more details it's hard to provide a more precise solution.
Also, ensure that the version of Pex you are using is compatible with the version of the .NET framework you are using.
Hope this helps in solving your issue.

A: Have you tried this? I have used the below attributes for testing the ArrayList. Perhaps these provide useful hints for your specific issue.
[PexMethod(OutputBehavior = OutputBehavior.FuzzOnly)] // The fuzz only setting tells Pex to make a best-effort and allow the method to throw an exception.
[PexAllowedException(typeof(ArgumentException))] // Pex will allow the method to throw this exception.

Also add the following attribute to have your test run against the assembly where your class resides. 
[PexAssemblyUnderTest("ReferencedAssembly.dll")]

Ensure that the exception(s) you are trying to account for get caught either within your ArrayList operations, or caught by your Pex method boundary (i.e., your test methods). Try to also check if your code includes a [PexClass] on the same assembly.
Your code might be a bit too complex for Pex to handle. Consider simplifying your test cases or your ArrayList usage. Maybe a simple integer/boolean/object array can suffice to test your code's logic.
You can also try using the Pex API. Here is a link with an example Pex API usage:
http://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/cambridge/projects/pex13/
Let me know if this helps.

A: There appears to be a compatibility issue of sorts between Pex and the ArrayList class.
Although it is important to note that the ArrayList class is not fully integrated with Pex, so even minor changes can produce extremely unpredictable behavior because of the way Pex is able to explore and infer.
It does seem that the problem might be in the Pex configuration, so here are some suggestions:


*

*Check that the Pex configuration that is referenced correctly points to the right assembly (the assembly where you placed the array list).

*Ensure that your test code is inside a test project (even if you reference the array list from the production code).

*Try refactoring the test so that Pex is directly testing all classes and not just the wrapper.

*Dispose objects that you are done with as soon as possible


In summary, Pex is not entirely suitable for everything and it might be more appropriate in some situations than others, especially when dealing with third-party libraries or types where it might not behave as expected. For a more robust testing approach, consider alternative approaches such as a testing framework or code analysis tool, or write--------------------
        }
    }

}//Assets/Scripts/Dice.cs
using System.Collections;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using UnityEngine;

namespace DiceGame
{
    public class Dice : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private List<Transform> diceSides;
        private int _rollValue;

        // The dice roll animation that is triggered when dice is thrown
        private Animation _diceAnim;

        public int RollValue { get => _rollValue; }

        private void Start()
        {
            _diceAnim = GetComponent<Animation>();
        }

        public void RollTheDice()
        {
            StartCoroutine(RollDice());
        }

        private IEnumerator RollDice()
        {
            // Rolling dice with animation
            _diceAnim.Play("DiceRoll");

            // Waiting for animation to end
            yield return new WaitForSeconds(_diceAnim.clip.length);

            // Giving random value to the dice
            _rollValue = Random.Range(1, 7);
            
            // Showing dice value
            ShowFaceOfDice();
        }

        private void ShowFaceOfDice()
        {
            for (int i = 0; i < diceSides.Count; i++)
            {
                if(i + 1 == _rollValue)
                {
                    // Show the correct dice side
                    diceSides[i].gameObject.SetActive(true);
                }
                else
                {
                    // Hide other dice sides
                    diceSides[i].gameObject.SetActive(false);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

//Assets/Scripts/Player.cs
using System.Collections;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using UnityEngine;
using UnityEngine.UI;
using TMPro;
using DiceGame;

public class Player : MonoBehaviour
{
    public Dice Dice;
    public int Score { get; private set; }
    public TextMeshProUGUI ScoreText;

    // When throwing a dice
    public delegate void OnThrowDice(int diceScore);
    public static event OnThrowDice OnDiceThrown;

    // When dice is rolled and its value is more than 1:
    public delegate void OnScored(int gainedScore);
    public static event OnScored OnScoreGained;

    public void ThrowDice()
    {
        Dice.RollTheDice();
        OnDiceThrown?.Invoke(Score);
        HandleScoring(Dice.RollValue);
        ScoreText.text = "Score: " + Score;
    }

    private void HandleScoring(int diceValue)
    {
        if(diceValue > 1)
        {
            Score += diceValue;
            OnScoreGained?.Invoke(diceValue);
        }
    }
}

//Assets/Scripts/UIManager.cs
using System.Collections;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using UnityEngine;
using UnityEngine.UI;
using DiceGame;

public class UIManager : MonoBehaviour
{
    public GameObject EndGameScreen;
    public Text EndGameText;

    private Player _player;

    private void Awake()
    {
        _player = FindObjectOfType<Player>();
    }

    private void Start()
    {
        // Subscribing to Player's events
        Player.OnDiceThrown += HandleDiceThrown;
        Player.OnScoreGained += HandleScoreGained;
    }

    private void HandleDiceThrown(int scoreBeforeThrow)
    {
        if(_player.Score >= 100)
        {
            EndGame("You win!");
        }
    }

    private void HandleScoreGained(int gainedScore)
    {
        if(_player.Score >= 100)
        {
            EndGame("You win!");
        }
    }

    private void EndGame(string message)
    {
        // Unsubscribing from Player's events to prevent triggering after--------------------
	}
	}    
}

