A: This exception might occur if you are using certain features which are not fully supported by Pex, specifically features of .NET that rely on Runtime serialization. 
If you are using the Pex's generated Code Contracts, they rely on a lot of features that might cause this exceptions like serialization and more especially in complex scenarios where you might use third-party libraries that rely on Runtime serialization e.g. Autofac, Entity Framework, etc. 
If you are using any of these, you should check if you can refactor your code to avoid these features or if these libraries have any way of supporting Pex's Code Contracts and you can avoid these kinds of exceptions.
The other option might be upgrading your Pex version to a newer one where this kind of problems have been fixed.
I hope these suggestions are helpful for you. If not, please provide more context about your issue.
Regards,
Abdul S

A: I think there might be some confusion in your question because all of the code snippets you've posted are about contracts, not the actual exception. 
The error message is about 'System.PexAssemblyInfo' not being serializable, but this doesn't seem to match the code snippets you've posted. It's unusual that you would get a serialization error in this context, as 'System.PexAssemblyInfo' is automatically generated by Pex and should not have any impact on the serialization.

Also, 'System.Runtime.Serialization.Formatters.Binary.ObjectReader' on type 'System.PexAssemblyInfo' is not marked as serializable

Could it be that your Pex version is somehow incompatible with the version of the .NET framework you're using? Or maybe there's a bug in the combination of the Pex version and the .NET version you're using? 

A: The error can also stem from a configuration issue. Check your NUnit test runner setup. In some situations, the NUnit test runner tries to serialize test fixtures and might run into issues with serializable types. Depending on your setup--------------------